{
  "id": "quiz-factorising-harder-quadratics",
  "title": "Quiz: Factorising Harder Quadratics (ax² + bx + c)",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Factorise $2x^2 + 7x + 3$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$(2x + 3)(x + 1)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$(2x + 1)(x + 3)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$(x + 3)(2x + 1)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$(2x + 3)(x + 3)$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "Multiply $a \\times c = 2 \\times 3 = 6$. Find two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to 7: that is 1 and 6. Split: $2x^2 + x + 6x + 3 = x(2x+1) + 3(2x+1) = (2x+1)(x+3)$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Factorise $3x^2 - 11x - 4$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$(3x - 1)(x + 4)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$(3x - 4)(x + 1)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$(3x + 4)(x - 1)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$(3x + 1)(x - 4)$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "d",
      "explanation": "$ac = 3 \\times (-4) = -12$. Two numbers that multiply to $-12$ and add to $-11$: $+1$ and $-12$. Split: $3x^2 + x - 12x - 4 = x(3x+1) - 4(3x+1) = (3x+1)(x-4)$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Factorise $6x^2 + x - 2$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$(6x - 1)(x + 2)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$(2x + 1)(3x - 2)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$(6x + 1)(x - 2)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$(2x - 1)(3x + 2)$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "d",
      "explanation": "$ac = 6 \\times (-2) = -12$. Two numbers that multiply to $-12$ and add to $1$: $+4$ and $-3$. Split: $6x^2 + 4x - 3x - 2 = 2x(3x+2) - 1(3x+2) = (3x+2)(2x-1) = (2x-1)(3x+2)$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "What is the first step in factorising $4x^2 - 8x - 12$ using the grouping method?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "Take out a common factor of 4 first"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "Complete the square"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "Divide everything by $x$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "Find two numbers that multiply to $4 \\times (-12)$ and add to $-8$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "a",
      "explanation": "Always check for a common factor first. All three terms are divisible by 4: $4x^2 - 8x - 12 = 4(x^2 - 2x - 3) = 4(x - 3)(x + 1)$. This makes the factorisation much simpler."
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Factorise $5x^2 - 20$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$5(x - 2)(x + 2)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$5(x - 4)(x + 4)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$5(x^2 - 4)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$(5x - 4)(x + 5)$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "a",
      "explanation": "First take out the common factor of 5: $5(x^2 - 4)$. Then recognise the difference of two squares: $x^2 - 4 = (x-2)(x+2)$. Fully factorised: $5(x-2)(x+2)$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Factorise $2x^2 - 5x - 3$.",
